Sure, here's a simple Rust implementation of a Connect Four game on a 4x4 board. This code provides a basic structure for the game, including a board representation, move handling, and a check for winning conditions. It's a starting point that you can expand upon with additional features like a graphical interface or AI opponents.

```rust
const BOARD_SIZE: usize = 4;

struct Board {
    cells: [[Option<Player>; BOARD_SIZE]; BOARD_SIZE],
}

enum Player {
    X,
    O,
}

impl Board {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Board { cells: [[None; BOARD_SIZE]; BOARD_SIZE] }
    }

    fn make_move(&mut self, col: usize, player: Player) -> Result<(), &'static str> {
        if col >= BOARD_SIZE {
            return Err("Column index out of bounds");
        }
        for row in (0..BOARD_SIZE).rev() {
            if self.cells[row][col].is_none() {
                self.cells[row][col] = Some(player);
                return Ok(());
            }
        }
        Err("Column is full")
    }

    fn check_winner(&self) -> Option<Player> {
        for player in [Player::X, Player::O] {
            for row in 0..BOARD_SIZE {
                if self.cells[row].iter().all(|&p| p == Some(player)) {
                    return Some(player);
                }
            }
            for col in 0..BOARD_SIZE {
                if self.cells.iter().all(|row| row[col] == Some(player)) {
                    return Some(player);
                }
            }
        }
        None
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut board = Board::new();
    let mut current_player = Player::X;

    loop {
        println!("Player {}, enter your move (0-3):", current_player);
        let mut input = String::new();
        std::io::stdin().read_line(&mut input).unwrap();
        let col: usize = input.trim().parse().unwrap();

        match board.make_move(col, current_player) {
            Ok(_) => {
                if let Some(winner) = board.check_winner() {
                    println!("Player {} wins!", winner);
                    break;
                }
                current_player = match current_player {
                    Player::X => Player::O,
                    Player::O => Player::X,
                };
            }
            Err(e) => println!("{}", e),
        }
    }
}
```

This code sets up a 4x4 board and allows two players to take turns placing their pieces. It checks for a winner after each move. You can further develop this by adding more sophisticated game logic, such as checking for diagonal wins or implementing a graphical user interface.
